DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppaamm__rraaddiiuuss module provides authentication services based upon the RA‐
     DIUS (Remote Authentication Dial In User Service) protocol for the PAM
     (Pluggable Authentication Module) framework.

     The ppaamm__rraaddiiuuss module accepts these optional parameters:

     uussee__ffiirrsstt__ppaassss
                 causes ppaamm__rraaddiiuuss to use a previously entered password in‐
                 stead of prompting for a new one.  If no password has been
                 entered then authentication fails.

     ttrryy__ffiirrsstt__ppaassss
                 causes ppaamm__rraaddiiuuss to use a previously entered password, if
                 one is available.  If no password has been entered,
                 ppaamm__rraaddiiuuss prompts for one as usual.

     eecchhoo__ppaassss   causes echoing to be left on if ppaamm__rraaddiiuuss prompts for a
                 password.

     ccoonnff=_p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e
                 specifies a non-standard location for the RADIUS client con‐
                 figuration file (normally located in _/_e_t_c_/_r_a_d_i_u_s_._c_o_n_f).

     nnaass__iidd=_i_d_e_n_t_i_f_i_e_r
                 specifies a NAS identifier to send instead of the hostname.

     nnaass__iippaaddddrr[=_a_d_d_r_e_s_s]
                 specifies a NAS IP address to be sent.  If option is present,
                 but there is no value provided then IP address corresponding
                 to the current hostname will be used.

     tteemmppllaattee__uusseerr=_u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e
                 specifies a user whose passwd(5) entry will be used as a tem‐
                 plate to create the session environment if the supplied user‐
                 name does not exist in local password database.  The user
                 will be authenticated with the supplied username and pass‐
                 word, but his credentials to the system will be presented as
                 the ones for _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e, i.e., his login class, home directory,
                 resource limits, etc. will be set to ones defined for
                 _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e.

                 If this option is omitted, and there is no username in the
                 system databases equal to the supplied one (as determined by
                 call to getpwnam(3)), the authentication will fail.

     nnoo__rreeppllyy__mmeessssaaggee
                 suppress printing of the contents of any RReeppllyy--MMeessssaaggee at‐
                 tributes found in AAcccceessss--AAcccceepptt and AAcccceessss--RReejjeecctt responses.
                 These are normally conveyed to the user as either informa‐
                 tional or error messages, depending on whether the access re‐
                 quest was accepted or rejected.

     nnoo__wwaarrnn     suppress warning messages to the user.  These messages in‐
                 clude reasons why the user's authentication attempt was de‐
